<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dagbon</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1896 CE (approx.) · capital Yendi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest kingdom of northern Ghana, ruled by the Ya Na from Yendi, whose drummers preserve one of Africa's richest oral histories.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dagbon traces its royal house to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-gbewaa">Na Gbewaa</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamprugu">Mamprugu</a>, through his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitobu">Sitobu</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-nyagse">Na Nyagse</a>, probably in the 15th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagomba-people">Dagomba</a> horsemen conquered the local peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-volta">White Volta</a> basin, killing or displacing their earth-priests and installing princes as chiefs. Later pressure from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gonja">Gonja</a> pushed the capital east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ya Na ruled through a hierarchy of chieftaincies held by royal sons and grandsons, who competed to rise toward the paramountcy. Royal drummers, the lunsi, recited genealogies and history at festivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was adopted at court under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-zangina">Na Zangina</a> in the early 18th century, and Muslim traders linked Dagbon to the kola routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and the forest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1744 Dagbon was defeated by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> and paid an annual tribute of slaves. In 1896 German forces defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagomba-people">Dagomba</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adibo">Adibo</a> and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>, and in 1899 Dagbon was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">British Gold Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a>, separating Yendi from its western lands. The Ya Na still reigns at Yendi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana's</a> Northern Region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-dagbon.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Dagbon" width="138" height="139"><figcaption>Image: Sintinon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Dagbon_(Northern_Territories)_locator_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yendi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Na Nyagse, Na Zangina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Dagomba traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dagbani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana, Togo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dagbon, Dagomba kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
